    interpretiveNotes: [{ note: `Guptavati-adi sapta-tika edition (814-pp PDF, per source-provenance table): Adhyaya 1 at PDF pp.116-215. This entry preserves the vulgate mantra-ganana numbering 1.66-67 for what the print counts as true vv.1.53-54 (vishveshvarim jagaddhatrim ... nidram bhagavatim / tvam svaha tvam svadha ... tridha matratmika sthita); true mula at PDF pp.182-186 with seven glosses pp.182-186. PDF lineation preserved above — rishi-vakya into brahmovaca speaker line, two padas per verse. The 53-54 vs 66-67 variance is mantra-ganana numbering (uvaca counted), not a different text; translation and Sadhana-Samara alignment are untouched.` }, { note: `Tika order on true vv.1.53-54: (1) Guptavati, (2) Chaturdhari, (3) Shantanavi (vishveshvari-sukta = ratri-sukta; kalaratrir maharatrir iti ratri-padabhyasat), (4) Nagojibhatti (vishveshvari-padenopakramat vishveshvari-suktam; asya devyah samutpattish caritam stotram iti), (5) Jagaccandrachandrika (mantra-counts 67-70), (6) Damshoddhara, (7) Chandraprabha Hindi. English digest only; verbatim mula not reproduced here.` }],
    translation: `Lord Brahmā praised Her, the Queen of the cosmos, the supporter of the worlds, the cause of preservation and destruction, the peerless Yoga-Nidrā of Lord Viṣṇu: "You are Svāhā, You are Svadhā, You are the sacred Vaṣaṭkāra; You are the imperishable nectar of the Threefold Sacred Syllables (A-U-M)!"`,
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
*Prathama Carita* represents **Brahma-Granthi Bheda** (piercing the knot of inertia and initial cosmic creation).
1. **Relationship to the Bhagavad Gītā**: The Bhagavad Gītā forms the golden foundation (*Akṣaya-bhitti*) of spiritual life, representing the sādhana of the mental sheath (*Manomaya Koṣa*). The *Devī Māhātmya* is the incomparable palace (*Prāsāda*) erected atop it, representing the sādhana of the wisdom sheath (*Vijñānamaya Koṣa*). In the Gītā one bathes in the ocean of Brahman; in Caṇḍī one achieves total, indivisible merger with the Divine Mother.
2. **Madhu and Kaiṭabha**: Emerged from the ear-wax of Viṣṇu (the subtle secretions of auditory and mental sensory impressions). *Madhu* represents the sweet, seductive inertia of the senses (*Tamas* disguised as pleasure). *Kaiṭabha* represents violent, rigid ego-obstinacy (*Rajas*). Together they attempt to devour Brahmā (spiritual aspiration).
3. **Yoga-Nidrā**: Viṣṇu cannot awaken until *Yoga-Nidrā*—the conscious withdrawal of Godhead—is praised and drawn out from His senses. When the Divine Will awakens, the demons are slain upon His thighs (firm spiritual grounding).

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
Brahma's direct hymn of praise to the sleeping Goddess, asking her to release Vishnu from yoga-nidra, is treated by Pradipa as the first instance in the text of a deva addressing Devi as supreme rather than as subordinate consort or shakti of a male deity - a theological move the commentary treats as the Saptashati's foundational claim. Guptavati cross-references the praise-vocabulary here with formulas used later in the great Devi-sukta of chapter 5, suggesting the text is deliberately training the reader's ear for the fuller hymn to come. Nagojibhatti resolves several compound epithets describing the Goddess as simultaneously world-supporter and world-dissolver, insisting the grammar supports reading both functions as literally simultaneous, not sequential. The Hindi Candraprabha keeps the register devotional, letting the hymn read as prayer rather than argument, appropriate to its function within the narrative as an actual invocation Brahma is uttering.`,
    keyPoints: ["**Brahma-Granthi Bheda**: Overcoming the first great knot of primal ignorance and inertia.", "**Gītā and Caṇḍī**: The Gītā prepares the mind; Caṇḍī dissolves individual authorship into the Mother's dynamic power.", "**Madhu and Kaiṭabha**: Slaying sensual sweetness and mental obstinacy."]
