    interpretiveNotes: [{ note: `Guptavati-adi sapta-tika edition (814-pp PDF, per source-provenance table): Adhyaya 1 at PDF pp.116-215. This digest entry highlights the battle close (true vv.1.68-78) from the full close at PDF pp.201-211 (v.68 p.201 vishnoh prabodhanarthaya, vv.69-70 p.203, v.71 p.204 krodha-rakta, v.72 pp.205-206 panca-varsha-sahasrani bahu-praharanah, v.73 p.206 tavapyatibalonmattau, v.74 p.207 bhavetam adya me, v.75 p.208 vancitabhyam, v.76 pp.209-210 avam jahi na yatrovi, v.77 pp.210-211 tathen, v.78 pp.211-212 evam esha samutpanna) with seven glosses per verse, plus Adhyaya-colophons and mantra-vibhaga at pp.212-215. PDF lineation for the highlighted padas is preserved above — rishiruvaca / bhagavanuvaca speaker lines, two padas per verse. The 85-104 numbering is mantra-ganana (78 shlokas + uvaca = 104: shlokanam ashtasaptatyash caturbhir adhikam shatam uvaca-vacanaih sardham, Jagaccandra p.213); translation and Sadhana-Samara alignment are untouched.` }, { note: `Tika order on each verse 1.68-78: (1) Guptavati (bahupraharanottaram dvau shlokau kvacit pathyete fn. on 72; janghane tatra jalabhavat on 77; mantra-vicara with apekshan-anapeksha caturvidha on 78), (2) Chaturdhari, (3) Shantanavi, (4) Nagojibhatti (saptashati-mantra-sankhya-puraniyataya uktih on 77; shuddha-mayikatva on 78 with Lakshmi-tantra Yoganidra quote), (5) Jagaccandrachandrika (mantra-counts 93-104: krodharakta 93, panca-varshe 94, uktavantau 95, bhagavanuvaca 96-98, rishir 99-102, avam jahi 101, tathen 103, evam esha 104; plus vibhaga karikas pp.213-214), (6) Damshoddhara/Damshoddhara, (7) Chandraprabha Hindi. English digest only; verbatim mula not reproduced here.` }],
    translation: `Thus praised, dark Night withdrew from Viṣṇu’s eyes-mouth-nostrils-arms-heart-breast and showed Herself to Brahmā. Freed, the Lord rose, left Yoga-sleep, fought the demons 5000 years arm-to-arm. Drunk with power, deluded by Mahāmāyā, they told Viṣṇu “ask a boon!” Smiling He said “be slain by Me — what other boon?” Seeing world flooded, they said “slay where earth is not flooded.” “So be it,” He placed them on His thighs and beheaded them with discus. Thus was Night born of Brahmā’s praise.`,
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
1. **Withdrawal through senses**: Yoganidrā exits exactly where She entered — eyes (seeing), mouth-nose (tasting-breathing), arms-heart-breast (grasping-feeling). Sādhanā reverses this: withdraw Mother-power from outer sensing to awaken Will.
2. **5000-year arm-fight**: No outer weapon works on deep inertia-obstinacy; only prolonged self-effort (bāhu-yuddha) matures. Deluded by Mahāmāyā they offer a boon to their foe — ego blinded offers its neck. Viṣṇu’s smile (vihasya) = compassion, not mockery.
3. **Thighs (jaghana) trick**: “Not flooded” = firm ground beyond flux. Thighs = grounded tapas, ethical seat. Placing demons on thighs = bringing tamas-rajas onto firm practice-ground, then discus (sudden viveka-chakra) severs heads (ego-centres). Brahma-granthi pierced; tamas lifts; higher awareness stirs. Here ends Prathama Carita (Mahākālī).

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
The climactic verses in which Night personified withdraws from Vishnu's body and Madhu-Kaitabha are finally slain receive close attention for the curious detail that the demons are killed 'on Vishnu's thighs', a detail Pradipa explains through the Puranic account (also referenced earlier in the commentary) that the demons, boon-protected from dying on land or water, are tricked into being slain on ground that is technically neither. Guptavati and Nagojibhatti both linger on this etymological point since it resolves an apparent narrative oddity that a casual reading might miss. The Hindi Candraprabha explains the trick plainly for readers unfamiliar with the boon's exact wording. Every commentator treats this episode as establishing the template the entire text will repeat: an apparently invincible demon is not defeated by superior force alone but by a wisdom that finds the exact condition his own boon left unguarded - a pattern that recurs, in different form, against Mahisha and later Shumbha-Nishumbha.`,
